1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:06,560
I think we can all agree that this game is missing something quite crucial, something fundamental and

2
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:08,330
absolutely necessary to all games.

3
00:00:08,330 --> 00:00:10,640
And that is score.

4
00:00:10,640 --> 00:00:16,130
Because how are you going to flex to your friends when you can't even show them a high score?

5
00:00:16,890 --> 00:00:23,790
In putting in a scoring feature, you will be learning one of the most fundamental programming concepts

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:29,640
and you'll be using a code block we have not touched yet, and that is variables.

7
00:00:29,670 --> 00:00:32,340
So are you ready to continue with your learning adventure?

8
00:00:32,550 --> 00:00:38,400
Looking at our game, what we want is a little score at the top that indicates how many times we have

9
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:42,270
headed the ball, and we want the score to reset if we miss.

10
00:00:42,890 --> 00:00:48,620
So moving over to the variables code group, you can see that there are actually not that many code

11
00:00:48,620 --> 00:00:49,520
blocks here.

12
00:00:49,820 --> 00:00:54,530
And for code blocks that are considered so important, you might be thinking this area should be stacked.

13
00:00:55,230 --> 00:01:01,140
Well, unlike all the other code block groups that we've looked at with variables, you can actually

14
00:01:01,140 --> 00:01:06,330
make more code blocks by clicking on this button here, which we will look at in a minute.

15
00:01:06,330 --> 00:01:10,890
But before we do this, I just want you to understand what variables are.

16
00:01:11,220 --> 00:01:16,350
Now, in programming, we often need to store information so that we can use it later on.

17
00:01:16,350 --> 00:01:20,130
And we also need a way to get this information easily.

18
00:01:20,520 --> 00:01:22,230
This is where variables come in.

19
00:01:22,260 --> 00:01:27,420
Variables are like containers that hold information and that's why they called variables.

20
00:01:27,420 --> 00:01:31,950
It basically means the container can hold various types of information.

21
00:01:32,520 --> 00:01:37,560
We can give variables names so that we can easily access them and put information into them, and use

22
00:01:37,560 --> 00:01:39,960
the information wherever we need it.

23
00:01:40,320 --> 00:01:45,990
Think of variables like boxes that we can store things in, just like you might have a box to store

24
00:01:45,990 --> 00:01:50,790
your games, a box to store your books, a box to store your old socks, or whatever.

25
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:56,970
Variables are pretty much the same, but instead of storing physical objects like books and games and

26
00:01:56,970 --> 00:02:02,670
stinky old socks, variables store data like numbers and text.

27
00:02:03,430 --> 00:02:04,780
So picture it like this.

28
00:02:05,360 --> 00:02:13,910
We have a variable called score, and inside this variable we are storing the value zero.

29
00:02:14,640 --> 00:02:21,210
But when I play gets a header, we go back, look inside our variable box scored score to discover what

30
00:02:21,210 --> 00:02:23,790
value is currently inside the variable.

31
00:02:23,790 --> 00:02:26,670
And so in this case when we look inside it, it's zero.

32
00:02:27,060 --> 00:02:32,790
Now that we've looked inside the box and discovered it's zero, we can simply add one which gives us

33
00:02:32,790 --> 00:02:35,070
one and store that back into the box.

34
00:02:35,750 --> 00:02:40,370
Then we can display our variable and the score of one is displayed on the screen.

35
00:02:40,790 --> 00:02:44,030
When we get another header, what does the program do?

36
00:02:44,060 --> 00:02:47,240
It'll go back, look inside the score box.

37
00:02:47,240 --> 00:02:50,540
And this time it'll see that it has number one stored in there.

38
00:02:50,750 --> 00:02:54,050
We can simply add one to this which now gives us two.

39
00:02:54,080 --> 00:02:58,910
Store that back inside our variable and display that on the screen.

40
00:02:58,910 --> 00:03:00,320
And obviously it goes on and on.

41
00:03:00,320 --> 00:03:06,140
But basically I want you to understand how variables are just digital storage boxes with names.

42
00:03:06,140 --> 00:03:11,750
And these named variables can store any number of text and numbers etc...

43
00:03:11,750 --> 00:03:15,350
And we can easily check what values our variables have.

44
00:03:15,350 --> 00:03:20,690
And we can use our variables in a number of ways, such as keeping our score for our game or even in

45
00:03:20,690 --> 00:03:26,630
invisible behind the scenes way, like increasing the difficulty of the game as we all look at later

46
00:03:26,630 --> 00:03:27,140
on.

47
00:03:27,410 --> 00:03:33,830
So now that you understand the basic idea of variables, let's take a look at these blocks and see how

48
00:03:33,830 --> 00:03:35,210
they relate to what we said.

49
00:03:36,180 --> 00:03:42,030
Firstly, remember how I said the variables group lets you create new blocks?

50
00:03:42,390 --> 00:03:46,740
Well, to do that we just need to click on this button and this makes a new variable.

51
00:03:46,740 --> 00:03:51,300
So basically a new box to store our digital information.

52
00:03:51,360 --> 00:03:54,330
And as you can see it asks what we want to call the variable.

53
00:03:54,450 --> 00:03:56,670
Because this is going to hold our score information.

54
00:03:56,670 --> 00:03:58,710
We will call it score.

55
00:04:00,150 --> 00:04:01,710
Then just below this.

56
00:04:01,710 --> 00:04:03,690
See how it gives us a decision?

57
00:04:04,140 --> 00:04:08,640
Is this variable for all sprites or is it for this sprite only?

58
00:04:08,670 --> 00:04:15,540
Now basically what this means is can all the other sprites see and modify our score variable, or can

59
00:04:15,540 --> 00:04:17,730
only this sprite access it?

60
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:21,420
So in programming this is what we call scope.

61
00:04:21,420 --> 00:04:26,910
And that's just like a fancy word for what part of the code can see or modify variables.

62
00:04:26,910 --> 00:04:33,630
If all sprites can see and modify the variable, that is called a global scope, while this sprite only

63
00:04:33,630 --> 00:04:38,130
can see and modify it, that is a local scope variable.

64
00:04:39,220 --> 00:04:42,100
We will choose the sprite only as our other sprite.

65
00:04:42,100 --> 00:04:48,070
Don't need to have access to our score variable and all the modifications can happen inside the sprite.

66
00:04:48,940 --> 00:04:50,320
And clicking okay.

67
00:04:50,320 --> 00:04:53,560
Here you can see our brand new score variable appearing here.

68
00:04:53,560 --> 00:04:58,030
And look how you can even see it on the screen, which in this case works perfectly for us.

69
00:04:58,240 --> 00:05:03,730
So now that we have our variable, I'm going to drag each of these blocks out so you can see what they

70
00:05:03,730 --> 00:05:04,390
do.

71
00:05:04,980 --> 00:05:08,610
So firstly we have set the variable to a number.

72
00:05:08,880 --> 00:05:13,200
And if you change this number and click it, well nothing happens.

73
00:05:13,200 --> 00:05:16,530
And that's just because we haven't selected our score variable.

74
00:05:17,620 --> 00:05:23,200
Doing so you will now see our score is set to three as reflected on the screen.

75
00:05:23,770 --> 00:05:29,830
So this block over here doesn't care what our variable was, it just sets it to whatever value we want.

76
00:05:29,830 --> 00:05:32,740
And this could even be a word like hi.

77
00:05:32,890 --> 00:05:34,780
But we want to keep it as a number.

78
00:05:34,780 --> 00:05:37,270
So let's set it back to zero.

79
00:05:38,400 --> 00:05:39,960
Moving down to our next variable.

80
00:05:39,960 --> 00:05:42,450
This one is to change the value.

81
00:05:42,480 --> 00:05:47,580
So if we were to put one here and then click see our score increases by one.

82
00:05:47,700 --> 00:05:51,630
And basically what this block is doing is what I explained earlier.

83
00:05:51,630 --> 00:05:57,060
When I click it it looks at what value is stored in our score variable, which in this case is two.

84
00:05:57,090 --> 00:06:00,690
And then it looks at what value it needs to be changed by which is one.

85
00:06:00,690 --> 00:06:02,700
So it takes two, adds one.

86
00:06:02,700 --> 00:06:03,990
And you've done the math in your head.

87
00:06:03,990 --> 00:06:04,620
Very good.

88
00:06:04,620 --> 00:06:06,390
And we get a score of three.

89
00:06:07,390 --> 00:06:12,250
And finally looking down, we have these two blocks which are quite simple.

90
00:06:12,280 --> 00:06:16,660
We can show a variable on our stage or we can hide it well.

91
00:06:16,660 --> 00:06:17,950
That's all there is to it.

92
00:06:18,100 --> 00:06:23,800
And with that, you now understand the fundamentals of variables and programming, which is a super,

93
00:06:23,800 --> 00:06:25,030
super important concept.

94
00:06:25,030 --> 00:06:30,670
And I want you to try now and see if you can implement the score variable into your code.

95
00:06:30,670 --> 00:06:34,390
And in the next lesson I will show you how we did it.

